00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:03.510 align:start position:0%
 
hi there steve kaufman here  today i want to talk about older  language learners  and the reason for that is that um  you know i'm i'm in i'm connected to  these various social networking sites  like uh i don't know uh facebook and  twitter and now google plus and so every  so often you see these things people  that you were following and they're  following you and so there was a message  there from someone saying you know i  want to  do some research on the problems of  older language learners and see what i  can do to help them  so i said what so i posted a little  comment there i said what do you  consider to be an older language learner  and he answered over 40.  so i said what makes you think that  people over 40 have particular problems  in language learning what do you think  these problems might be  and the answer was well you know they  might develop hearing loss or something  of that nature  and i must say i was completely taken  aback by this comment  uh i think that  i don't know that over 40 qualifies as  old  but if i if i  assume that over 60 does that's me  uh i think i'm a better language learner  now than i was when i was 16.  and  all the evidence that i've seen in the  reading that i've done  is that our cognitive abilities don't  really start to decline until  well past the mid 70s  and if we work hard at maintaining these  cognitive abilities by keeping our minds  active  you know i'm sure that they  our cognitive abilities and our ability  to create new neurons and neural  networks remains quite strong  now i can't compare  the ability of us the average 60 year  old with the average  uh 15 year old but everything i've seen  suggests to me that  that obviously a child before the age of  say 10  seems to have a major advantage in  language learning  and there are probably a number of  reasons i think as a very young child of  course the brain  is still flexible enough so that the  brain hasn't sort of formed around  sort of one language so it's much more  open to new languages  as the brain sort of because necessarily  the brain has to form  patterns has to form rules for itself so  that that it can deal with all of the  the experiences and the phenomenon that  it's it's phenomena that it's  confronted with so the the positive side  of this is that as we grow older we have  more patterns in place we have more  experience to draw on we have more  wisdom in a sense we've experienced more  things but we perhaps become less open  to new things  and that's i think what happens on  language learning we're open to any  language when we're born we could learn  any language as a native language and as  we develop therefore this these sets of  patterns to deal with our native  language we perhaps become less open to  new languages and i think their  young people who study two or three  languages have a big advantage but once  we pass the age of 10 or so i think the  brain is more or less formed from what  i've read  and then if i look at young learners the  majority by the way the majority of  teens are not interested in language  learning and don't do well the majority  that i've seen at least in canada  but on the other hand  for example at linc and on the recent  hangouts that i've been conducting in  google plus we've had some young people  show up who are extremely good  and  and i think some of the more  enthusiastic  so-called polyglots people learning  different languages probably are younger  but it's their enthusiasm  and their willingness to put in the time  and the effort and their the fact that  they aren't resisting the language  these are the sort of attitudinal  factors that enable them to be  successful and there's no reason why an  older person can't have the same  attitude i like to feel that when i  study a language i'm totally  enthusiastic about the language i put in  the time necessary i don't resist the  language i don't question  you know why do they say it this way and  wouldn't it be better if they said it  the way we said it in our language  none of these things and i think it may  be true to some extent that um  some people do this but i think young  people do this as well  so  uh  you know i i i  and as for hearing loss like i know i  amongst my friends i don't know of many  of them yeah you do lose the sort of  high frequency range of hearing but not  to the point where it would affect your  ability to hear the language uh now  there are people with hearing aids and  who had developed you know  significant hearing loss i have a very  good friend who's 83 and who has a  hearing aid doesn't hear very well but  he's very enthusiastic learning spanish  and having a great time uh and and so so  i just i felt this like don't ma like  old people aren't in any way  uh disadvantaged older people whether  over 40 as this person had it or over 60  are not handicapped people  and in terms of their cognitive  abilities they're just as good  as younger people  if they have the same attitude if they  have the attitude of not resisting the  language of being caught up in the in  the excitement of learning a new  language  uh not resisting the language if they  can visualize themselves speaking that  other language  if they have these attitudes they can be  just as good as anyone else it is true  that in our society  uh  you know again i was at a meet-up the  other evening here in vancouver it was  styled as a  meetup of language enthusiasts and  linguists  and someone sort of made the statement  well we all know that our ability to  learn language declines with age and as  the older we get the harder it is to  learn and i said no i'm sorry that's not  been my experience well i mean you can't  say that because everybody knows that  the older you get the harder it is to  learn the language  and i think there are a lot of people  who glibly toss these ideas around and  unfortunately i think it influences  some  people who are sort of past their 30s  they think they can't learn a language  anymore nothing could be further from  the truth and  you know it's not just learning  languages you know if i look at my wife  for example who's so enthusiastic about  her piano she plays two three hours a  day she doesn't have a teacher but she's  improving by leaps and bounds  i mean we can learn anything we want at  any age as long as we have the attitude  of a learner the attitude of a learner  is someone who is independent  who doesn't expect someone else to teach  them or to give it to them who's  enthusiastic who's confident and who is  determined  to get there and also someone who isn't  too concerned about comparing themselves  to other people now my wife plays the  piano she doesn't care whether she's  better than someone else i know very  well that there's all kinds of polyglots  on the internet that are better than i  am in a number of languages if not all  that doesn't diminish my enjoyment  one little bit and every one of us as we  learn a language whatever our age and  whatever our opportunities to speak and  so forth  we can all enjoy the progress that we're  making and that's all we need to achieve  is to achieve that little bit of  progress that gives us a sense of  satisfaction we don't have to compare  ourselves to anyone else  um so yeah just to say that  i think there's a bit of a a prejudice  uh and one that's that's that works to  the advantage of of some people who give  up on the op on the possibility that  they could learn one two or more  languages uh you know past their teens  i can assure you that  i mean i learned czech from from  basically a standing start except that i  had done russian within a year i would  never have considered that possible uh  when i was in my 20s you know putting in  an hour an hour and a half or so  eventually two hours a day  and and the reason again it gets back to  you as we're older and as we've done  these things we get better at doing them  we know how to do them again the brain  develops these patterns these these  routines  that  it's no longer a new phenomenon now  someone who has never learned a second  language or has only had say school  exposure to french or  who grew up in china who learned english  for 10 years and can't speak english i  mean they have no sense of what it's  like to transform themselves into a  speaker of a second language so that  they have they have an attitude they're  defeated before they start but that's  the attitude that's not the age and if  they  accept the fact that they can learn that  it's it's a gradual process you have to  do it with enthusiasm and determination  i don't see any difference between a 16  year old and a 66 year old so there's my  take on  language learning and  and older people thank you for listening  bye for now

